<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Kokand Autonomy</h1><p class="st-meta">1917 CE – 1918 CE · capital Kokand</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived Muslim autonomous government in Turkestan, set up by Jadid reformers in 1917 and crushed by Soviet forces months later.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent-soviet">Tashkent Soviet</a>, dominated by Russian settlers, excluded Muslims from power. In November 1917 the Fourth Extraordinary Regional Muslim Congress met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Kokand</a> and proclaimed the Turkestan Autonomy within a future democratic Russian federation.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The government, led first by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammadjan-tynyshbaev">Muhammadjan Tynyshbaev</a> and then by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-chokay">Mustafa Chokay</a>, included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jadidism">Jadid</a> reformers and some Russian members. It planned a constituent assembly and a militia, but lacked money and troops and depended on the support of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Kokand</a> population and local strongmen.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In February 1918 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-guards-russia">Red Guards</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenian-revolutionary-federation">Armenian Dashnak</a> units from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a> stormed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Kokand</a>, killing thousands and burning much of the city. The government collapsed, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-chokay">Chokay</a> fled into exile. Its destruction helped spark the Basmachi resistance against Soviet rule.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kokand-autonomy.jpg" alt="Kokand Autonomy" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Turkestan Autonomy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Turkestan_(Kokand)_Autonomy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1917 CE – 1918 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kokand</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Muhammadjan Tynyshbaev, Mustafa Chokay</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Uzbek, Persian, Kazakh</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, <a href="/countries/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a>, <a href="/countries/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Turkestan Autonomy, Turkistan Muxtoriyati</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1918&amp;focus=kokand-autonomy" aria-label="Kokand Autonomy on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6421 730 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kokand Autonomy lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6741 986L5635 424M6741 986L6691 960"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5635 424h0M6691 960h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6741 986h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6741 986h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1918&amp;focus=kokand-autonomy">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
